From a29c296ced0afb6479c07027c4c80db58ca163bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:30:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] FEXCore: read guest code fault-safely while compiling

The frontend reads guest code with plain loads after checking the
executable range, while holding the code-invalidation lock. Another
guest thread can revoke the page between the check and the read: a guest
mprotect or munmap changes the host protection first and invalidates
translated code afterwards, and the multiblock decoder reads ahead into
pages the guest has not executed (branch targets up to 4 pages on). The
load then faults inside FEX. A synchronous fault is not deferred, so it
reached the guest with the compiler's frames below it; a guest handler
that resumes elsewhere abandons those frames, and the lock stays
read-held: the next invalidation waits forever and every compile queues
behind it.

THE FINALS' anti-cheat re-protects its code pages while other threads run
next to them and froze this way after 8 to 16 minutes: 67 threads waited
on CodeInvalidationMutex, whose word read 0x40010001 (one read owner,
one write waiter, readers waiting).

Copy guest code through FaultSafeRead (a byte loop whose load the
frontend's SIGSEGV handler resumes as a failed copy, like
FaultSafeUserMemAccess::CopyFromUser) in the decoder (PeekByte,
ReadData), for the SMC CRC of an instruction, and for the disk cache's
code hashes and stored copy. A read that faults is a byte that cannot be
fetched: the decoder ends the block there (a fetch fault only if the
guest really executes it, as on hardware), the CRC never matches, and
the disk cache skips the block.

Test: eac-tests/decode-race stalled in 2 of 3 runs before, passes 5 of 5
after (8.6 to 10.3 million protection toggles per run).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
